{"count": 26451, "next": "https://api.acmi.net.au/creators/?page=408", "previous": "https://api.acmi.net.au/creators/?page=406", "results": [{"id": 81361, "name": "Sharon Stone", "roles_in_work": [{"id": 2476414, "title": "Last dance", "work_id": 95562, "role": "cast", "role_id": 33, "is_primary": false}], "also_known_as": "Sharon Vonne Stone", "date_of_birth": "1958-03-10", "date_of_death": "", "genders": [{"id": 5, "name": "Female", "slug": "female"}], "languages": [{"id": 1, "name": "English", "slug": "english"}], "places_of_operation": [{"id": 1, "name": "United States of America", "slug": "united-states-of-america"}], "biography": "Sharon Vonne Stone (born March 10, 1958) is an American actress. Known for primarily playing femme fatales and women of mystery on film and television, she became one of the most popular sex symbols of the 1990s. She is the recipient of various accolades, including a Primetime Emmy Award, a Golden Globe Award, and a nomination for an Academy Award. She received a star on the Hollywood Walk of Fame in 1995 and was named Officer of the Order of Arts and Letters in France in 2005 (Commander in 2021).After modeling in television commercials and print advertisements, Stone made her film debut as an extra in Woody Allen's dramedy Stardust Memories (1980) and played her first speaking part in Wes Craven's horror film Deadly Blessing (1981). In the 1980s, she appeared in such pictures as Irreconcilable Differences (1984), King Solomon's Mines (1985), Cold Steel (1987), and Above the Law (1988). She had a breakthrough with her part in Paul Verhoeven's science fiction action film Total Recall (1990), before rising to international recognition when she portrayed Catherine Tramell in another Verhoeven film, the erotic thriller Basic Instinct (1992), for which she earned her first Golden Globe Award nomination for Best Actress in a Motion Picture – Drama.\nStone's performance as a trophy wife in Martin Scorsese's epic crime drama Casino (1995) earned her the best reviews of her career, which garnered her a Golden Globe Award along with an Academy Award nomination for Best Actress. Her other notable films include Sliver (1993), The Specialist (1994), The Quick and the Dead (1995), Sphere (1998), The Mighty (1998), The Muse (1999), Catwoman (2004), Broken Flowers (2005), Alpha Dog (2006), Bobby (2006), Lovelace (2013), Fading Gigolo (2013), The Disaster Artist (2017), Rolling Thunder Revue: A Bob Dylan Story by Martin Scorsese (2019), and The Laundromat (2019).\nOn television, Stone has had leading and supporting roles in productions such as the ABC miniseries War and Remembrance (1987), the HBO television film If These Walls Could Talk 2 (2000), Steven Soderbergh's Mosaic (2017) and Ryan Murphy's Ratched (2020). She made guest appearances in The Practice (2004) and Law & Order: Special Victims Unit (2010), winning the Primetime Emmy Award for Outstanding Guest Actress in a Drama Series for the former.", "biography_author": "Wikidata", "date_of_biography": "2023-09-06T01:13:46.184817+10:00", "external_links": [{"id": 5151, "uri": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sharon_Stone"}], "uuid": "f2ac046a-c553-490f-8955-d84c0702aefa", "source": {"name": "Vernon", "slug": "vernon"}, "source_identifier": "38128", "external_references": [{"id": 25150, "source": {"name": "Wikidata", "slug": "wikidata"}, "source_identifier": "Q62975"}, {"id": 31492, "source": {"name": "VIAF", "slug": "viaf"}, "source_identifier": "61688646"}, {"id": 31493, "source": {"name": "LOC Auth", "slug": "loc-auth"}, "source_identifier": "n95111574"}, {"id": 31494, "source": {"name": "WorldCat", "slug": "worldcat"}, "source_identifier": "lccn-n95111574"}], "date_modified": "2023-09-06T01:13:48.329550+10:00"}, {"id": 81360, "name": "Reinhold Schunzel", "roles_in_work": [{"id": 2107149, "title": "Balalaika", "work_id": 95560, "role": "director", "role_id": 938, "is_primary": true}], "also_known_as": "", "date_of_birth": "1886-11-07", "date_of_death": "1954-11-11", "genders": [{"id": 9, "name": "Male", "slug": "male"}], "languages": [{"id": 5, "name": "German", "slug": "german"}], "places_of_operation": [{"id": 18, "name": "Germany", "slug": "germany"}], "biography": "Reinhold Schünzel (7 November 1888 – 11 November 1954) was a German actor and director, active in both Germany and the United States. The son of a German father and a Jewish mother, he was born in St. Pauli, the poorest part of Hamburg. Despite being of Jewish ancestry, Schünzel was allowed by the Nazis to continue making films for several years until he left in 1937 to live abroad.", "biography_author": "Wikidata", "date_of_biography": "2023-08-01T16:43:18.870040+10:00", "external_links": [{"id": 2923, "uri": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Reinhold_Sch%C3%BCnzel"}], "uuid": "86dc0bfa-05f5-4f70-9d63-230794162834", "source": {"name": "Vernon", "slug": "vernon"}, "source_identifier": "27246", "external_references": [{"id": 12431, "source": {"name": "Wikidata", "slug": "wikidata"}, "source_identifier": "Q28998"}, {"id": 12432, "source": {"name": "VIAF", "slug": "viaf"}, "source_identifier": "69127222"}, {"id": 12433, "source": {"name": "LOC Auth", "slug": "loc-auth"}, "source_identifier": "n90669371"}, {"id": 12434, "source": {"name": "WorldCat", "slug": "worldcat"}, "source_identifier": "lccn-n90669371"}], "date_modified": "2023-09-06T00:25:08.938978+10:00"}, {"id": 81359, "name": "Harold Lowenstein", "roles_in_work": [{"id": 918102, "title": "London's country", "work_id": 95556, "role": "co-director", "role_id": 959, "is_primary": true}], "also_known_as": "", "date_of_birth": null, "date_of_death": null, "genders": [], "languages": [], "places_of_operation": [], "biography": "", "biography_author": null, "date_of_biography": null, "external_links": [], "uuid": "db4e6407-da3c-403e-be4c-e54c9b7583a0", "source": {"name": "Vernon", "slug": "vernon"}, "source_identifier": null, "external_references": [{"id": 41539, "source": {"name": "Wikidata", "slug": "wi